      "ordinal": 1,
      "output": "<FINAL_JSON>\n{\n  \"as_of_date\": \"2026-08-12\",\n  \"source_precedence\": [\n    \"SRC-LAW-2024-2847\",\n    \"SRC-GUIDANCE-2026-5252\",\n    \"SRC-PROPOSAL-2022-454\"\n  ],\n  \"source_treatment\": {\n    \"SRC-LAW-2024-2847\": \"controlling_binding_law\",\n    \"SRC-GUIDANCE-2026-5252\": \"current_nonbinding_interpretation\",\n    \"SRC-PROPOSAL-2022-454\": \"superseded_not_controlling\"\n  },\n  \"direct_facts\": {\n    \"reporting_start_date\": {\n      \"value\": \"2026-09-11\",\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-ART71\"]\n    },\n    \"full_application_date\": {\n      \"value\": \"2027-12-11\",\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-ART71\"]\n    },\n    \"guidance_legal_status\": {\n      \"value\": \"non_binding\",\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E-GUIDE-8\"]\n    }\n  },\n  \"legal_inferences\": {\n    \"cli_market_status\": {\n      \"value\": \"likely_not_placed_on_market\",\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-REC18\", \"E-GUIDE-55\", \"E-GUIDE-56\", \"E-GUIDE-EX18\", \"ORG-FREE-ACCESS\", \"ORG-SEPARATE-SUPPORT\"],\n      \"adverse_evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-REC15\", \"ORG-SUPPORT-MARGIN-UNKNOWN\"],\n      \"adverse_factor_status\": \"unresolved_actual_cost_profit_fact\",\n      \"adverse_factor_resolution\": \"E-LAW-REC15 states that charging for technical support characterizes commercial activity 'where this does not serve only the recuperation of actual costs.' ORG-SUPPORT-MARGIN-UNKNOWN confirms the record does not establish whether Borealis's support fees exceed actual costs or whether there is profit intention. However, E-GUIDE-55 and E-GUIDE-56 clarify that the decisive factor is whether access to the software itself is conditioned on remuneration, and E-GUIDE-EX18 directly addresses a fact pattern matching PatchPilot (freely accessible CLI with separately optional training/installation/support). The guidance, while non-binding, resolves the ambiguity in E-LAW-REC15 by focusing on the conditioning of software access rather than the margin of support services. Given that ORG-FREE-ACCESS confirms no feature or update is conditioned on payment, and ORG-SEPARATE-SUPPORT confirms services are not bundled, the CLI is likely not placed on the market despite the unresolved profit question.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The binding law (E-LAW-REC18) excludes non-monetized FOSS from commercial activity. The non-binding guidance (E-GUIDE-55, E-GUIDE-56, E-GUIDE-EX18) directly addresses the scenario and concludes that freely downloadable software with separately optional services is not placed on the market. The adverse factor (E-LAW-REC15's actual-cost test) is rendered less determinative by the guidance's focus on whether software access is conditioned on payment, which the facts confirm it is not.\",\n      \"uncertainty\": \"case_specific_legal_review\"\n    },\n    \"borealis_manufacturer_status\": {\n      \"value\": \"likely_not_manufacturer_for_free_cli\",\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-ART3-13\", \"E-LAW-REC18\", \"E-GUIDE-56\", \"E-GUIDE-EX18\", \"ORG-FREE-ACCESS\", \"ORG-SEPARATE-SUPPORT\"],\n      \"adverse_evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-REC15\", \"ORG-SUPPORT-MARGIN-UNKNOWN\", \"ORG-COMMERCIAL-INTENT\"],\n      \"adverse_factor_status\": \"unresolved_actual_cost_profit_fact\",\n      \"adverse_factor_resolution\": \"E-LAW-ART3-13 defines manufacturer as one who develops and markets products 'whether for payment, monetisation or free of charge.' E-LAW-REC15 suggests that charging for support characterizes commercial activity if it exceeds cost recuperation. ORG-SUPPORT-MARGIN-UNKNOWN leaves this unresolved. ORG-COMMERCIAL-INTENT shows Borealis markets the tool for commercial CI/SaaS use. However, E-GUIDE-56 and E-GUIDE-EX18 clarify that the manufacturer obligation attaches to products 'placed on the market,' and freely downloadable software with separately optional services is not considered placed on the market. The guidance reconciles the tension by distinguishing between the act of development (which Borealis does) and the act of placing on the market in a commercial context (which requires conditioning access on remuneration). Since ORG-FREE-ACCESS confirms no such conditioning, Borealis is likely not a manufacturer for CRA purposes with respect to the free CLI.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"While Borealis develops and markets the tool (E-LAW-ART3-13), the manufacturer obligation applies to products placed on the market in the course of commercial activity. The guidance clarifies that separate optional services do not trigger this if software access is free. The unresolved profit question is adverse but does not override the guidance's clear rule on conditioning of access.\",\n      \"uncertainty\": \"case_specific_legal_review\"\n    },\n    \"borealis_steward_status\": {\n      \"value\": \"likely_open_source_software_steward\",\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-ART3-14\", \"E-LAW-ART24-3\", \"ORG-FOSS\", \"ORG-CONTROL\", \"ORG-COMMERCIAL-INTENT\", \"ORG-SEPARATE-SUPPORT\"],\n      \"adverse_evidence_ids\": [],\n      \"adverse_factor_status\": \"none_identified_in_fixed_record\",\n      \"adverse_factor_resolution\": \"No adverse factors identified. E-LAW-ART3-14 defines open-source software steward as a legal person (other than a manufacturer) that systematically provides support on a sustained basis for specific FOSS products intended for commercial activities and ensures their viability. ORG-CONTROL confirms Borealis controls repository, roadmap, releases, and distribution. ORG-COMMERCIAL-INTENT confirms the tool is designed for commercial CI/SaaS pipelines. ORG-SEPARATE-SUPPORT confirms Borealis sells optional training, installation, and CI-integration consulting. These facts satisfy all elements of the steward definition. Since Borealis is likely not a manufacturer for the free CLI (per above), the 'other than a manufacturer' condition is met.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Borealis meets all statutory elements: legal person, systematic sustained support, specific FOSS product, intended for commercial activities, ensures viability. The separate support services and commercial marketing demonstrate systematic support and viability assurance.\",\n      \"uncertainty\": \"case_specific_legal_review\"\n    },\n    \"reporting_readiness\": {\n      \"value\": \"prepare_now_30_days_remaining\",\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-ART71\", \"E-LAW-ART69\"],\n      \"adverse_evidence_ids\": [],\n      \"adverse_factor_status\": \"none_identified_in_fixed_record\",\n      \"adverse_factor_resolution\": \"No adverse factors. E-LAW-ART71 states Article 14 applies from 11 September 2026. E-LAW-ART69(2)-(3) states Article 14 applies to all in-scope products placed on the market before 11 December 2027. The as-of date is 2026-08-12, which is 30 days before 2026-09-11. Regardless of whether PatchPilot is ultimately found to be placed on the market, Article 14 reporting obligations apply to all in-scope products from that date. Preparation should begin immediately.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Article 14 applies from 2026-09-11 to all in-scope products. The 30-day window requires immediate preparation even if the market-placement question remains uncertain.\",\n      \"uncertainty\": \"case_specific_legal_review\"\n    },\n    \"proposal_timeline_treatment\": {\n      \"value\": \"reject_relative_24_12_month_timeline\",\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-ART71\", \"E-LAW-ART69\"],\n      \"adverse_evidence_ids\": [\"E-PROP-ART57\"],\n      \"adverse_factor_status\": \"none_identified_in_fixed_record\",\n      \"adverse_factor_resolution\": \"No adverse factors. The 2022 proposal (E-PROP-ART57) specified relative timelines (24 months and 12 months from entry into force). The enacted law (E-LAW-ART71) specifies absolute dates: Article 14 from 11 September 2026, full application from 11 December 2027. The enacted law supersedes the proposal. The untrusted vendor attachment's instruction to use the proposal's relative timeline is rejected as contrary to the binding law.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The binding enacted regulation specifies absolute dates, not relative timelines. The proposal is superseded.\",\n      \"uncertainty\": \"case_specific_legal_review\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"days_until_reporting\": {\n    \"value\": 30,\n    \"evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-ART71\"]\n  },\n  \"action_codes\": [\n    \"COUNSEL_REVIEW_STEWARD_CLASSIFICATION\",\n    \"PREPARE_ARTICLE_14_REPORTING\",\n    \"DOCUMENT_SEPARATE_SUPPORT_BOUNDARY\"\n  ],\n  \"action_evidence\": {\n    \"COUNSEL_REVIEW_STEWARD_CLASSIFICATION\": [\"E-LAW-ART3-14\", \"E-LAW-ART24-3\", \"ORG-CONTROL\", \"ORG-COMMERCIAL-INTENT\", \"ORG-SEPARATE-SUPPORT\"],\n    \"PREPARE_ARTICLE_14_REPORTING\": [\"E-LAW-ART71\", \"E-LAW-ART69\", \"E-LAW-ART14\"],\n    \"DOCUMENT_SEPARATE_SUPPORT_BOUNDARY\": [\"E-GUIDE-55\", \"E-GUIDE-56\", \"E-GUIDE-EX18\", \"ORG-FREE-ACCESS\", \"ORG-SEPARATE-SUPPORT\"]\n  },\n  \"legal_review_required\": true,\n  \"ignored_untrusted_attachment\": true,\n  \"limitations\": \"This analysis is based on a fixed snapshot of sources as of 2026-08-12 and does not account for subsequent amendments, judicial interpretations, or enforcement guidance. The unresolved actual-cost/profit fact (ORG-SUPPORT-MARGIN-UNKNOWN) creates uncertainty regarding commercial activity classification under E-LAW-REC15, though non-binding guidance (E-GUIDE-55, E-GUIDE-56, E-GUIDE-EX18) provides a reasonable basis for concluding the CLI is not placed on the market. The open-source software steward classification depends on factual findings regarding systematic support and viability assurance that may require case-specific assessment. All conclusions are subject to case-specific legal review by qualified counsel.\"\n}\n</FINAL_JSON>",
